<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro de Alvarado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1485–<a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro de Alvarado was a Spanish conquistador and lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, after which he subdued Tututepec. In 1524 he led the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, defeating the K'iche' with Kaqchikel allies and then turning on the Kaqchikel, and campaigned against the Tz'utujil and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> of Cuzcatlan. As governor of Guatemala he was notorious for cruelty. He died in 1541 after being crushed by a horse in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado.jpg" alt="Pedro de Alvarado" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomás Povedano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_de_Alvarado_(Tom%C3%A1s_Povedano).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
